A||||5||c m west||Has anyone measured the  bumpsteer on the rear of pre-88||||1||
Z||000000||c m west||04-01-2001||07:02 PM||||just curious<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||04-01-2001||08:14 PM||||I doubt it. doing something like that reliably requires rather expensive tools. The simplest way I can think of requires an alignment rack and the removal of the rear springs so you can easily move thru the range of travel of the rear a arm while the alignment machine measures the changes. (With the springs on you'd have to put a hell of allot of weight on the rear.)<P>Frankly I don't think the bump steer is as bad as many people have made it out. Once you lock in the cradle so it can't dance all over it settles allot of things. I had to relearn how to drive my car after installing polly cradel bushings last year. (And that's the only change I made...)<P>Eric, keep in mind that many of the people who "fixed" this also change some to all the rear bushings and struts at the same time. That makes the end result invalid in determining the problem.<P>I'm leary of most of the aftermarket fixes for this problem. The ones that scare me the most are the jobs that tie the steering arm on the spindle to the lower A arm in some way. If I was going to do any of them, I'd use that one BatBoy got that corrects the length of the tie rod. If not that exact one then something along that line.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 04-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||c m west||04-01-2001||08:35 PM||||At a given ride height of 14" (measured from the center of the wheel to the edge of the fenderwell) we have found that the wheel will tow in an average of .020" for every 2 inches of travel up and and average of .070" for every 2 inches down - so for all intensive perposes there is little to NO bumpsteer - pictures of out bumpsteer guage in use will be posted on our website.<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||04-01-2001||08:39 PM||||Hi Theogre,<P>I was think about the same thing on the 84-87. Move the tie-rod mount point to the lower control arm. <P>So what is the hazard in doing this? I've thought about it and I don't see an immediate problem but if it's so good, why didn't GM do it?<P>Something makes me concerned about transmitting all of the shock to the lower control arm even if the bushings are Herb Adams spherical bearings or the like.<P>What's your concern?<P>Terry<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||rockcrawl||04-01-2001||09:39 PM||||You will never eliminate bumpsteer by only changing the length of the tie rod. With a double wishbone suspension yes, but not with a strut suspension. The only way to correct it is to move the tie rod to same plane as the lower control arm. The inner pivot also needs to be on the same axis as the control arm, or attached to the control arm. That is basicly what Ryane has done. Examine their setup and you will understand. That setup will have absolutely no bumpsteer. Also look at independent rear suspensions that were designed as such. They differ greatly from the Fiero design.<BR> If Eric's measurements and Ogre's speculations are correct, there is no need to correct any bumpsteer.||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||04-02-2001||07:42 PM||||I'd have to see raynes set in use. It just looks like it binds the suspension on the pictures I've seen.<P>I've driven the hell out of mine. drive even harder now that the polly stablized the cradle. And I still need new shocks and struts. (I think these are the original struts on it)<P>The only time I get anything significant in the way of a "bump" steer is when I go over somehting big like a set of RR tracks that cross at an angle. With some of the really crappy grades around here you're going to get that with almost any car.<P>Other wise I've found the thing to be much more stable than most people say they are. But then I also have an 87SC with the "heavy duty" suspension in it. If this thing was any stiffer you'd need a neck brace to drive it.<P>I'm still maintaining that the first single change people make is polly or solid cradel bushings. then figure out what else to do. I still intend to replace the a arm bushings with OE rubber. (That won't happen till next year at this rate...)||||reg||1
